== 7.1 First Aid and Scene Safety

*First aid* is the immediate assistance given to a person who is ill or injured until further care is available or the situation is resolved. Its purpose is to support life, prevent further harm and provide reassurance. It does not replace emergency services or clinical treatment.

Before approaching, check for dangers such as traffic, fire, electricity, unstable structures or hazardous substances. Do not enter an unsafe area. Call local emergency services when needed, describe the location and situation, and follow the dispatcher's instructions. Use protective barriers when available and act within your training. Ask for consent when the person is able to respond; follow applicable local procedures.

#rule-box("Protect yourself first")[
  Do not touch a person who is still in contact with a live electrical source. Arrange for the power to be disconnected safely and call for help. A rescuer who becomes injured cannot assist effectively.
]

== 7.2 Responsiveness and Breathing

An unresponsive person who is not breathing normally needs immediate emergency help. Call local emergency services and follow the dispatcher's instructions. Begin cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) as directed and use an automated external defibrillator (AED) as soon as one is available, following its prompts. CPR procedures can differ by age and situation; formal training and local guidance are important. Do not delay emergency help while searching a textbook.

If a person is unresponsive but breathing normally, contact emergency services and monitor breathing. A recovery position may help maintain the airway when appropriate; if injury is suspected, avoid unnecessary movement and follow dispatcher guidance. If breathing becomes abnormal, tell the dispatcher and follow instructions. Do not force food, drink or medication into an unresponsive person's mouth.

