== 7.3 Bleeding, Burns and Fractures

For severe external bleeding, call emergency services and apply firm direct pressure to the bleeding wound with a dressing or available clean material, when safe and appropriate. Maintain pressure and follow dispatcher guidance. If an object is embedded in the wound, do not remove it; apply pressure around it rather than pressing on the object. A tourniquet may be used for life-threatening limb bleeding according to training and local guidance.

For a thermal burn, move away from the heat source safely. Cool the affected area under cool running water for 20 minutes where possible, while keeping the rest of the person warm. Remove nearby jewellery or clothing unless it is stuck to the skin; do not pull stuck material away. Do not apply ice, butter or unverified substances. Serious, large, deep, electrical or chemical burns require urgent assessment; follow local advice for the specific exposure.

For a suspected fracture, keep the injured area still and seek appropriate help. Do not force a limb straight or attempt to push a protruding bone back. If head, neck or spinal injury is suspected, avoid unnecessary movement unless needed for immediate safety, CPR or bleeding control, and follow dispatcher instructions.

#concept-box("Match the response to the situation")[
  A bleeding wound, a burn and a suspected fracture need different actions. Check scene safety and call for help; then use the appropriate response rather than applying one treatment to every injury.
]

== 7.4 Prevention and Preparation

Accident prevention involves recognising and managing hazards: maintain equipment, keep exits accessible, supervise activities appropriately and follow safety procedures. Store medicines and chemicals securely and in labelled containers. Avoid putting chemicals into food or drink containers. A first-aid kit and trained responders are useful, but equipment alone does not replace a safety plan or emergency services.

Review a school or workplace plan with participants: who calls for help, how to describe the location, where equipment is kept and how access needs are met. Local emergency numbers and response arrangements should be verified in advance. This chapter is a study summary, not a complete first-aid course.

== 7.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Scene safety", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [A person is in contact with a live electrical source. What is the safest initial principle? \
A. Do not touch them until the power is safely disconnected \
B. Grab them immediately with bare hands \
C. Ignore the electrical hazard \
D. Pour water over the equipment],
  [A live electrical source can injure a rescuer. Arrange safe disconnection and call for help. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Severe bleeding", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [For severe external bleeding without an embedded object, an appropriate general action is to \
A. ignore the wound \
B. call for help and apply firm direct pressure when safe \
C. wait without monitoring the person \
D. force the injured person to continue playing],
  [Emergency help and firm direct pressure can help control severe external bleeding. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "A burn", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [For a thermal burn, which action is appropriate when safe and feasible? \
A. Apply ice directly \
B. Pull off material stuck to the skin \
C. Cool the affected area under cool running water \
D. Apply butter as the first treatment],
  [Cool running water is appropriate for a thermal burn; avoid ice and do not pull away stuck material. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Unresponsive and not breathing normally", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A person is unresponsive and not breathing normally. What should a bystander do? \
A. Force them to drink \
B. Leave them alone without calling \
C. Delay until a textbook diagnosis is available \
D. Call emergency services and follow instructions for CPR and an AED],
  [This is an emergency. Call for help immediately and follow dispatcher or AED instructions, acting within training. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Do not move or feed automatically")[
  Unnecessary movement can worsen some injuries. Do not force food or drink into an unresponsive person. Follow emergency instructions and move only when necessary for safety or essential care.
]

